{
  "passage": "Sound is produced when an object vibrates. A vibration is a rapid to-and-fro motion. When you pluck the string of a sitar, the string vibrates and you hear a sound. When the vibration stops, the sound also stops. In human beings, sound is produced by the voice box, called the larynx, at the upper end of the windpipe. Two vocal cords are stretched across it, and air from the lungs makes them vibrate. Sound needs a medium to travel. It can travel through solids, liquids and gases, but it cannot travel through a vacuum. This is why astronauts on the Moon, where there is no air, talk to each other by radio. Sound travels faster in solids than in air. If you put your ear to one end of a long table and a friend taps the other end gently, you hear the tap clearly through the wood. The number of vibrations in one second is called the frequency, and it is measured in hertz. A high frequency gives a high-pitched sound, like a whistle, while a low frequency gives a low-pitched sound, like a drum. The human ear can hear sounds with frequencies from about 20 hertz to 20,000 hertz. Sounds below this range are called infrasonic, and sounds above it are called ultrasonic. Bats and dolphins can hear ultrasonic sounds.",
